","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS°)_products - Σ(n * ΔS°)_reactants

where n is the number of moles and ΔS° is the standard entropy change for each substance.

First, we need to find the standard entropies (ΔS°) for each reactant and product at 298 K:

ΔS°(CO) = 25.8 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O2) = 20.5 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 20.8 J/mol·K

The reaction equation is:



\[
\text{CO(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow \text{CO}_2(g)
\]

The balanced reaction for 3 moles of CO is:

\[
3\text{CO}(g) + 1.5\text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 3\text{CO}_2(g)
\]

Now, we can calculate the entropy change:

\[
\Delta S = 3 \times \Delta S°(\text{CO}_2) - (3 \times \Delta S°(\text{CO}) + 1.5 \times \Delta S°(\text{O}_2))
\]

\[
\Delta S = 3 \times 20.8 \text{ J/mol·K} - (3 \times 25.8 \text{ J/mol·K} + 1.5 \times 20.5 \text{ J/mol·K})
\]

\[
\Delta S = 62.4 \text{ J/mol·K} - (77.6 \text{ J/mol·K} + 30.75 \text{ J/mol·K})
\]

\[
\Delta S = 62.4 \text{ J/mol·K} - 108.35 \text{ J/mol·K}
\]

\[
\Delta S = -45.95 \text{ J/mol·K}
\]

The change in entropy for the reaction is \(\boxed{-45.95 \text{ J/K}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
