\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time}
\]
The length of the train is the distance it travels to reach the girl. The girl is 150 m away from the entrance of the train. The train is moving at 45 km/h and the girl is moving at 9 km/h. The relative speed of the train relative to the girl is:
\[
45 \text{ km/h} - 9 \text{ km/h} = 36 \text{ km/h}
\]
Convert the relative speed to m/s:
\[
36 \text{ km/h} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 100 \text{ m/s}
\]
The train travels 150 m to reach the girl, and then it must travel the length of the train to pass the girl. The total distance traveled by the train is the sum of the initial distance between the train and the jaler and the length of the train. The time taken is 25 seconds.

The distance traveled by the train is the sum of the initial distance (150 m) and the length of the train (L). The total distance is:
\[
150 + L
\]
The time taken is 25 seconds, and the relative speed is 100 m/s. Using the formula for distance:
\[
150 + L = 100 \times 25
\]
\[
150 + L = 2500
\]
\[
L = 2500 - 150 = 2350 \text{ m}
\]
Thus, the length of the train is \(\boxed{2350 \text{ m}}\).
