   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ km/hr} \times 3 \text{ hr} = 30 \text{ km}
   \]

2. **Determine the speed difference**:  
   The difference in speed is \( 20 \text{ km/hr} - 10 \text{ km/hr} = 10 \text{ km/hr} \).  
   This means that B travels 10 km farther than A per hour.

3. **Calculate the time it takes for B to catch A**:  
   Since B must travel 30 km to reach A's starting point, the time it takes for B to catch A is:  
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{10 \text{ km/hr}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ km/hr}} = 3 \text{ hr}
   \]

4. **Calculate the distance B travels to catch A**:  
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ km/hr} \times 3 \text{ hr} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]

   However, this distance is the distance B travels after of the 3 hours.  
   The total distance from the start is:  
   \[
   \text{Total distance} = 30 \text{ km} + 30 \text{ km} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]

