An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} \approx 90.0 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new voltage gain \( A_{vf'} \):**
   \[
   \text{Reduction in gain} = 6 \text{ dB} \implies \text{New gain} = 10^{-0.6} \times 100 = 100 \times 0.161 = 16.1 \text{ dB dB}
   \]
   \[
   A_{vf}' = 16.1 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \):**
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_{vf'}}{1 + m_v} = \frac{16.1}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{16.1}{1.1} \approx 14.7 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{14.7 - 90.0}{90.0} \right) \times 100 = \frac{-75.3}{90.0} \times 100 \approx -84.3\%
   \]

   **Final Answer:** The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-84.3\%}\).
