   The mass matrix is diagonal with each element equal to the mass of the tooth:  
   \[
   M = \begin{bmatrix} m_t & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & m_t & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & m_t \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.0114 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0.0114 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0.0114 \end{bmatrix}
   \]

2. **Stiffness Matrix \( K \):**  
   The stiffness matrix is sparse and sparse is symmetric. The stiffness matrix is:  
   \[
   K = \begin{bmatrix} k_t + K_c & -K_c & 0 \\ -K_c & k_t + K_c & -K_c \\ 0 & -K_c & k_t + K_c \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 430,000 + 45,430 & -45,430 & 0 \\ -45,430 & 430,000 + 45,430 & -45,300 \\ 0 & -45,430 & 430,000 + 45,400 \end{bmatrix} = \
   \begin{bmatrix} 475,430 & -45,430 & 0 \\ -45,430 & 475,430 & -45,430 \\ 0 & -45,430 & 475,430 \end{bmatrix}
   \]

3. **Eigenvalue Problem:**  
   The natural frequencies and mode shapes are found by solving the eigenvalue problem:  
   \[
   \lambda \mathbf{M} \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{K} \mathbf{v}
   \]  
   where \( \lambda \) are the eigenvalues (eigen roots) and \( \mathbf{v} \) are the mode vectors.  

4. **Solving the Eigenvalue Problem:**  
