## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m}
\]  
The force on the proton is given by:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
Substituting \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\):  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19}) \times (500) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}
\]  
Now, substitute \(F\) into the acceleration formula:  
\[
a = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27}} = 4.81 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

#### (b) The time taken to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is calculated using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
Since the proton starts from rest, \(u = 0\). Rearranging for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000}{4.81 \times 10^8} = 7.29 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]  
The time taken is \(\boxed{7.29 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

