","To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation states that time passes slower for a moving observer relative to an observer at rest. In this case, the spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth, so the passengers on the spaceship will appear to pass less time than someone on Earth.

However, the key to this problem is that both the in-cockot and the carterboard clocks are moving at the same speed relative to Earth. Therefore, they will experience time dilation at the same rate. Therefore, the time measured by both clocks will be the same when measured by an observer on Earth.

The time dilation formula is given by:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time experienced by the observer on Earth, t is the time experienced by the spaceship, and v is the relative velocity of the spaceship relative to Earth.

In this case, t = 3 years (the time on the clock in the cockpit), and v = 0.6c. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:

t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(0.64)
t' = 3 years / 0.8
t' = 3.75 years

So, when the ship returns to Earth, the clock on Earth will show a time of 3.75 years, and the clock in the cockpit will show a time of 3 years. Since both clock are at rest relative to the spaceship, they will show the same time when measured by an observer on the spaceship.

So, the time shown on the clock in the cabin when it is compared to a clock on Earth will be 3.75 years.",fluency,"# Task Description
