\[
\sigma_{vr} = \frac{G b^2}{2 \pi \sqrt{3} \sqrt{3r}}
\]  
where:  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(r\) is the radius of the hard particles.  

Given:  
- Diameter of the hard particles = \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m, so radius \(r = 3 \times 10^{-8}\) m  
- Average center-to-center spacing = \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\)  
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\sigma_{vr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9})^2}{2 \pi \sqrt{3} \times sqrt{3} \times 3 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{vr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 0.081 \times 10^{-18}}{2 \pi \times 3 \times 3 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{vr} = \frac{2.1 \times 10^{-7}}{2 \pi \times 9 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{vr} = \frac{2.1 \times 10^{-7}}{5.68 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{vr} = 37.1 \text{ MPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{37.1 \text{ MPa}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

